  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 AM EDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, the ridge over the Southeast starts to flatten \ntoday as a broad trough moves into the Midwest and Ohio Valley. At \nthe surface, a cold front will be over the Ohio Valley tonight. Dry \nweather will continue today through Thursday afternoon. Thursday \nevening and into the overnight hours, showers and storms will \nincrease, mainly in the northern third of the region as a decaying \ncold front slowly moves down from the north. A minor shortwave \nmoving through the pattern may also enhance activity. Perhaps a few \nstrong storms can't be ruled out in the evening hours but overall \ninstability will be decreasing significantly if this timing holds. \n\nDaily rain chances are expected mainly in the afternoon and evening \nhours from Friday through the weekend possibly into early next week. \nIn the upper levels, nearly zonal flow will be in place with minor \nshort waves moving through the pattern at times. The weak surface \nboundary will also be lingering over or near the region through the \nweekend. Showers and storms will likely be scattered at times. NBM \nhas the highest chances on Saturday afternoon. \n\nEarly next week, a ridge will be over the Southeast. Isolated to \nscattered showers and storms will be possible with the highest \nchances in and near the terrain. Afternoon highs will be above \nnormal throughout the forecast period with upper 80s expected near \nTri-Cities and lower 90s for Knoxville and Chattanooga areas, \ngenerally about 6 to 8 degrees above normal.",
